// English: Pawson framework (Unlocking the Old Testament lecture 1), Unlocking the Bible lectures (Revelation), Keller axis, issues
window.EN_PF={
src:"David Pawson, Unlocking the Bible (Chinese subtitled series), Lecture 1: Old Testament Overview",
periods:{
pre:{n:"Prehistory",sub:"Before Israel",y:"Undated",ev:"Creation, fall, flood, Babel",who:"All humanity",lead:"—",books:["Genesis 1–11"],s:"Genesis 1–11 is about the whole human race, before the chosen people appear: the creation of the universe, Eden, human sin, the flood and Babel. It is the background before Israel's history begins."},
p1:{n:"Election",sub:"2000 BC",y:"from c. 2000 BC",ev:"God chooses Abraham and his descendants",who:"Abraham",lead:"Patriarchs",leadW:"Abraham → Joseph",books:["Genesis 12–50","Job"],s:"Israel's history begins around 2000 BC when God chooses Abraham; the leaders are the patriarchs. Pawson thought Job may also come from this period, since its nomadic, herding life looks like that of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob."},
g1:{n:"400 years of silence",sub:"Egypt",y:"Between Genesis and Exodus",ev:"God is silent and does not act",world:"In this period the cultures of Egypt, India and China flourished.",s:"Four hundred years separate Genesis and Exodus; Exodus itself says so, though readers often miss it. God was completely silent and did nothing, so the Bible records nothing. Pawson's point: only God's history is worth our attention; when God is not busy, people are very busy."},
p2:{n:"Exodus",sub:"1500 BC",y:"from c. 1500 BC",ev:"Moses leads Israel out of Egypt",who:"Moses",lead:"Prophets",leadW:"Moses → Samuel",books:["Exodus","Leviticus","Numbers","Deuteronomy","Joshua","Judges","Ruth"],s:"Few books come from this period: Exodus, Leviticus, Numbers and Deuteronomy were written in Moses' time, and Joshua, Judges and Ruth record its history. Leadership shifts from patriarchs to prophets, from Moses to Samuel."},
p3:{n:"Kingdom",sub:"1000 BC",y:"from c. 1000 BC",ev:"The promised land possessed; peace and prosperity",who:"David",lead:"Kings",leadW:"Saul → Zedekiah",books:["Samuel","Kings","Chronicles","Psalms (David)","Song of Songs (Solomon)","Proverbs (Solomon)","Ecclesiastes (Solomon)"],s:"This is Israel's high point: not only its own land but many conquered nations. Culture and the arts flourish when a nation is rich and at peace, so the poetic books come from this golden age. After Solomon came civil war: ten northern tribes called themselves Israel, the two southern tribes Judah. Elijah and Elisha prophesied in this period but left no books in their names."},
p4:{n:"Exile",sub:"500 BC",y:"from c. 500 BC",ev:"The promised land lost; exile to Babylon",who:"Isaiah",lead:"Priests",leadW:"Joshua (returning with Zerubbabel) → Caiaphas",books:["The Prophets (before / during / after exile)","Daniel","Esther","Ezra","Nehemiah"],s:"Most of the prophetic books concern the exile: before it they warn the people they will lose the land, during it they comfort them, after the return they care for rebuilding. The people were away from home for seventy years. Daniel and Esther tell of life in exile in Babylon and Persia; Ezra and Nehemiah help rebuild Jerusalem and resettle the people. Leadership passes to the priests."},
g2:{n:"400 years of silence",sub:"Between the Testaments",y:"Between Malachi and Matthew",ev:"God is silent and does not act; the Apocrypha written",world:"Socrates, Plato and Aristotle, along with the Buddha, Confucius, Alexander the Great and Caesar: the \"great men\" Pawson lists fall roughly around this period.",s:"Four hundred years separate Malachi and Matthew; God was completely silent and worked no miracles. Books were written then (the Apocrypha, such as the history of the Maccabean revolt against Greek rule), but Pawson held they do not belong in the canon because God was not speaking or acting on earth when they were written: they are ordinary history, not prophetic history. After four hundred years God spoke again, and the first prophet was John the Baptist."},
nt:{n:"New Testament",sub:"Under 100 years",y:"c. 5 BC – AD 95",ev:"Jesus is born, dies, rises and ascends",who:"Jesus",lead:"Prophet, Priest, King",leadW:"Jesus holds all three offices",books:["The four Gospels","Acts","The Letters","Revelation"],s:"The Old Testament covers more than two thousand years; the whole New Testament was written within a hundred. Israel tried four kinds of leader, patriarch, prophet, king and priest, and all failed; what they were really looking for was one person who is prophet, priest and king at once."}},
canon:{heb:[
 {n:"Law (Torah)",note:"Named by their opening words; the synagogue reads it through every year",alt:{GEN:"In the beginning",EXO:"Names",LEV:"He called",NUM:"In the wilderness",DEU:"Words"}},
 {n:"Former Prophets",note:"Prophetic history: selects only what matters to God and shows how events relate to him",alt:{"1SA":"Samuel (one book)","1KI":"Kings (one book)"}},
 {n:"Latter Prophets",note:"Three major prophets plus the Twelve (one book)",alt:{}},
 {n:"Writings",note:"Ruth and Chronicles are not prophetic history; Daniel is also placed here",alt:{LAM:"How!",ECC:"The Preacher",EZR:"Ezra–Nehemiah (one book)","1CH":"Events of the days (one book)"}}],
 en:[{n:"History",note:"In chronological order, Genesis to Esther"},{n:"Poetry",note:""},{n:"Major Prophets",note:"\"Major\" and \"minor\" refer only to length, not importance"},{n:"Minor Prophets",note:""}]},
principles:[
"\"Bible\" (biblia) means \"books\": it is a library, not one book. God gave it book by book, so we should read it book by book, not verse by verse or topic by topic.",
"Every verse belongs to a book, and every book to a stretch of history. God always spoke at a particular time and place to a particular situation, so we need the setting first.",
"For geography you need only two maps: the Middle East (the Fertile Crescent) and the promised land. The land sits between the two great powers of Egypt and Mesopotamia, at the crossroads of the world; God put his people where everyone could see them.",
"All history is written by selection and connection. Prophetic history selects only what matters to God and shows how events connect with him, which is why the Buddha and Confucius never appear in the Bible, and why the Hebrew Bible calls Joshua to Kings the \"Former Prophets.\"",
"In our Bibles the prophets are arranged by length, not by date. Before reading one, find out whether it was written before, during or after the exile.",
"Pawson recommended The Daily Bible (NIV), edited by his friend F. LaGard Smith: no chapters or verses, arranged chronologically in 365 readings, so you can read God's story in a year."],
geo:[
"One end of the Fertile Crescent is the Nile, the other the Tigris and Euphrates (Mesopotamia, \"between the rivers\"); these were the two power centers of the ancient world: Egypt against Assyria and Babylon.",
"With the Arabian desert to the east, the Mediterranean to the west and hard black basalt to the north, armies and traders had to use the narrow coastal corridor past Megiddo (Armageddon).",
"Galilee in the north sat on the crossroads, which Pawson called \"Galilee of the nations\" (Isa 9:1, \"Galilee of the Gentiles\"); Nazareth overlooks it. Jerusalem in the southern hills was isolated, home to the Judeans. When John says \"the Jews\" killed Jesus, he means the people of Judea in the south; most Galileans supported Jesus.",
"This land, the size of Wales, is like the world in miniature: from the snows of Hermon to the depths of the Dead Sea, plants and animals of Europe, Asia and Africa are all found there."],
gaps:["400 years of silence (Egypt)","400 years of silence"]
};

window.EN_ZL={
lectures:{nt33:"New Testament, Lecture 33: Revelation (1)",nt34:"New Testament, Lecture 34: Revelation (2) The Ascended Lord",nt35:"New Testament, Lecture 35: Revelation (3) The Great Tribulation",nt36:"New Testament, Lecture 36: Revelation (4)",nt37:"New Testament, Lecture 37: Revelation (5) The Final Millennium",nt38:"New Testament, Lecture 38: Revelation (6)"},
era:{13:"Revelation (lectures 33–38): Pawson called this his favorite book and the one the church most neglects. The Bible runs from the beginning of the universe to its end, and Revelation is the end; what Satan least wants people to read are the first three and last three chapters of the Bible, one telling how he got in, the other how he is thrown out. He divides the book in four: things are bad on earth (chs. 2–3), good in heaven (chs. 4–5), worse on earth first (chs. 6–18), then completely good (chs. 19–22). The book prepares the church to \"overcome\" under persecution, not to satisfy curiosity about the future."},
views:{
revelation:"Pawson noted that Luther, Calvin and Zwingli thought little of this book and the church has long neglected it; yet it is one of the few books that begins by promising a blessing to readers and ends by warning against adding or removing anything. Two reading principles: first, it was written for ordinary people in seven churches (many of them slaves), so common sense will grasp the point; someone summed it up as \"Jesus wins,\" and he said that is right. Second, its purpose is practical: not a timetable of the end, but getting believers ready now to endure to the end (Rev 14:12). The setting is about AD 96, when Emperor Domitian required people to declare \"Caesar is Lord.\" The revelation passes from God → Jesus → the Spirit → an angel → John → the seven churches; John was like a stenographer, told eleven times to \"write,\" which is why the Greek is rough.",
"rev-churches":"The seven letters share one format: the church addressed, Jesus' self-description, praise, rebuke, counsel, \"he who has an ear, let him hear,\" and a promise to the one who overcomes; praise always comes before rebuke. Pawson said Satan is a creature and cannot be everywhere; his base on earth was Pergamum (\"where Satan's throne is\"). The churches nearest it, Pergamum and Thyatira, were infiltrated by idolatry and immorality; Smyrna and Philadelphia were persecuted by Jews and received only encouragement; the farthest, Ephesus and Laodicea, faced no attack yet one lost its first love and the other became lukewarm. The two outwardly most successful churches got no praise at all. A lampstand can be removed; churches do not last automatically. The promises are to individuals: in the letter to Laodicea, if just one person opens the door, the Lord comes in. He also noted that the overcomer's name will not be blotted out of the book of life, which is in reverse a warning: this was written to believers, who must not take \"once saved, always saved\" for granted.",
"rev-throne":"Revelation pulls back two curtains: one hiding heaven, one hiding the future. Chapter 4 is about the Creator and his creatures: God is still on the throne, history is not out of control, and Satan must get permission for everything he does. The twenty-four elders stand for the twelve tribes and the twelve apostles: Old and New Testament people are one flock in heaven. In chapter 5 John weeps because no one is found worthy to end history; the one slain yet alive, Pawson argued, should be pictured as a ram with seven horns, strong and powerful, not a meek little lamb. Only he is worthy to open the scroll and begin the process of ending history.",
"rev-tribulation":"The great tribulation lasts three and a half years (42 months, 1,260 days). The seals, trumpets and bowls each split 4, 2, 1: the first four together, then two, then the last alone, and the seventh in each is a worldwide earthquake. The three series are neither simply consecutive nor simply overlapping but \"consecutive yet simultaneous\": the first six of each happen in order, and the three sevenths end together, like a music building to a climax. After each sixth comes an interlude about believers (chs. 7, 10–11, 12–13); the great crowd of chapter 7 \"come out of the great tribulation,\" mostly as martyrs, so he did not believe believers will be raptured before it. He took the woman of chapter 12 as the church, not Mary or Israel; the \"anti\" in antichrist means \"in place of\" Christ. Of the four schools of interpretation, he found the preterist and historicist views hard to sustain, held mainly to the futurist view, and allowed some value in the idealist (symbolic) view. Who the two witnesses are, he said, we do not know and should not guess.",
"rev-babylon":"The second half of Revelation contrasts two women and two cities: the prostitute Babylon and the bride, the New Jerusalem. From Babel onward Babylon stands for pride, ambition and aggression. Pawson held that Revelation's Babylon is not Rome but the end-time global center of finance and pleasure, whose god is money; he was unsure where it would be, but said its spirit is already visible in today's cities. He expected Babylon to be brought down not directly by God but by politicians jealous of the merchants' power. Believers have three things to do with Babylon: expect it to persecute the saints; come out of it, as God commands, for safety rather than salvation, and on their own feet; and when it falls, sing Hallelujah.",
"rev-return":"Chapters 19–21 are seven visions introduced by \"I saw,\" and the chapter breaks scramble their order; read them as written: chapter 19 (the return) before chapter 20 (the millennium). The four enemies at the end, Satan, the antichrist, the false prophet and Babylon, are dealt with in the reverse order of their appearance: Babylon falls first, then the beast and false prophet are thrown alive into the lake of fire, and finally Satan is bound. The first time Jesus rode into the city on a donkey; this time he rides a white horse to make war and judge. Armageddon is Megiddo, the crossroads of Europe, Asia and Africa. On the rapture, he said the rapture is certain and only its timing is debated: he held a post-tribulation rapture, tracing the \"secret pre-tribulation rapture\" to Darby and the Plymouth Brethren in the 1830s and its spread in America through the Scofield Reference Bible, not to the historic church. Chapters 6–16 do not use the word \"church,\" but \"saints\" means Christians, and God can protect his people in the midst of wrath as he protected Israel in Goshen. Better to warn believers now to prepare for suffering, he said, than leave them unready.",
"rev-millennium":"Revelation 20 mentions \"a thousand years\" six times, and Pawson thought the text is clear enough: after Christ returns he will reign on earth with the overcoming saints for a thousand years. Those in the first resurrection are the ones faithful to Jesus who refused the beast's mark; the martyrs once executed by Rome now share his throne. So training today in church, at work and in character is preparation for ruling with him. Satan is released after the thousand years, he said, to prove that the root of sin is in the human heart, not in economics or environment. He sorted the views into three: amillennialism mostly comes from doubt or allegorizing the text; postmillennialism has a \"spiritual\" form that bends the text and a \"political\" form whose triumphalism does not fit reality; he himself held premillennialism, the view of the early church, from which Augustine turned under the influence of Greek thought.",
"rev-white-throne":"All the dead will rise with bodies, the righteous and the unrighteous in two resurrections a thousand years apart; both the Pharisees and Jesus accepted that the righteous rise first. Jesus will be the judge, and the books recording every word, deed and thought will be opened with nothing left out; believers' names must be in the book of life. Pawson rejected annihilationism, holding that Satan, the beast and the false prophet will be tormented day and night forever; the lake of fire is outside the city, and the cowardly, the faithless, the immoral and liars have their share there. He said Revelation was written to make sure believers' names stay in the book of life; in Hebrew and Greek \"faith\" and \"faithfulness\" are one word, so we must follow faithfully all our lives.",
newcreation:"Pawson said God will not scrap the universe and start again but \"recycle\" it and renew it; pollution is caused by people, so God recycles people first. Jesus' risen body is the first part of the old creation to be renewed, and new creation is like \"the eighth day,\" which is why the church worships on Sunday. Heaven is not our eternal home: believers who die are with the Lord, but the eternal home is the new earth; the New Jerusalem comes down from heaven to earth, God moving in with people rather than people moving in with God. He also discussed the city's size (about 1,500 miles in length, width and height) and listed ten reasons to read Revelation: to understand the end, guard against heresy, make sense of history, gain hope, spur evangelism, stir worship, resist worldliness, pursue holiness, prepare for persecution, and know Christ better. Today's disasters are the birth pains of a new world."},
ty:{"passover>rev-throne":"The Passover lamb → the slain Lamb standing at the center of the throne (Rev 5:6)","plagues>rev-tribulation":"The plagues of Egypt, with God protecting Goshen → the trumpets and bowls, with God keeping his people in the midst of wrath","babel>rev-babylon":"The pride of Babel → \"Fallen is Babylon the great\" (Rev 18:2)","triumphal-entry>rev-return":"Riding a donkey into the city in peace → riding a white horse to make war and judge (Rev 19:11)","ascension>rev-return":"\"This Jesus will come in the same way as you saw him go into heaven\" (Acts 1:11) → heaven opens and he comes with his armies","davidic-cov>rev-millennium":"David's heir on the throne forever → Christ reigning on earth with his saints"}
};

window.EN_KELLER={
acts:[{n:"Creation",q:"Where did we come from?",s:"God is a triune, relational God; the world was originally good, and humans bear God's image."},
{n:"Fall",q:"What went wrong?",s:"People made something other than God their god; sin brings bondage and condemnation, and people, communities and creation are all broken."},
{n:"Redemption",q:"What will put it right?",s:"God himself enters history and, through Christ's substitution, buys back a lost world."},
{n:"Restoration",q:"How will it end?",s:"Redemption is not escape from the world but its renewal: heaven comes down to earth and all things are made new."}],
era:{
0:{tb:["Jesus is the true and better Adam, who passed the test in the garden, and whose obedience is credited to us","Jesus is the true and better Abel, killed though innocent, whose blood cries out not for our condemnation but for our acquittal"],s:"Keller said many people shrink the gospel to two or three chapters: fall, redemption, heaven. But the Bible starts with creation, so the goal of redemption is not to escape the material world but to renew it."},
1:{tb:["Jesus is the true and better Abraham, who answered God's call to leave all that was familiar and go out to create a new people","Jesus is the true and better Isaac, not just taken up the mountain by his father but truly sacrificed","Jesus is the true and better Jacob, who wrestled with God and took the blow of justice we deserved","Jesus is the true and better Joseph, who at the right hand of the king forgives and saves those who betrayed him","Jesus is the true and better Job, the truly innocent sufferer, who intercedes for his foolish friends"],s:"The patriarchs were flawed: liars, favorites, schemers. Keller pointed out that the Bible is not a book of moral heroes but a book about God's grace; the hero of these stories is God, not the patriarchs."},
2:{tb:["Jesus is the true and better Moses, who stands in the gap between the people and God and mediates a new covenant","Jesus is the true and better rock of Moses, struck with the rod of God's justice, who gives us living water in the desert","Jesus is the true Passover lamb: judgment passes over us because it fell on him"],s:"In the exodus, redemption comes before the law. Keller often used this order to show the difference between religion and the gospel: religion says \"I obey, therefore I am accepted\"; the gospel says \"I am accepted, therefore I obey.\""},
4:{tb:[],s:"Judges grows darker as it goes, and the last judges are worse than the first. Keller said the book keeps showing that people need a Savior who will not fail, and that true Judge is Christ."},
5:{tb:["Jesus is the true and better David, whose victory becomes his people's victory, though they never lifted a stone to accomplish it"],s:"Keller often read David and Goliath as \"we are not David\": we are the trembling Israelites on the sidelines who need a representative to fight for us."},
6:{tb:["Jesus is the true and better Jonah, who was cast into the storm so that we could be brought in"],s:"The prophets keep calling the people back to the covenant. Reading Jonah, Keller pointed out that the prophet has both the younger son's rebellion and the elder son's self-righteousness, and both need grace."},
7:{tb:[],s:"Keller used Daniel to preach \"living by faith in a secular world\": the exiles lived in an empire that demanded assimilation, serving Babylon while refusing to bow to its gods."},
8:{tb:["Jesus is the true and better Esther, who did not just risk losing an earthly palace but lost the heavenly one, who did not just risk his life but gave it to save his people"],s:""},
10:{tb:[],s:"Keller said the Christmas story has \"hard edges\": Jesus was born into poverty, political violence and flight. The incarnation means God does not save from a distance but enters our condition himself."},
11:{tb:[],s:"Keller used the parable of the prodigal sons to explain Jesus' message: both sons leave home, one by indulgence, one by keeping the rules; both are trying to be their own saviors. The gospel is neither religion nor irreligion but a third way."},
12:{tb:[],s:"The cross is substitution: he took what we deserved and we receive what he deserved. Keller held that the resurrection is a testable historical claim, not just a spiritual symbol."},
13:{tb:[],s:"The church is a community renewed by the gospel, living for the good of its neighbors in the city. The story ends not with souls leaving the world but with a new heaven and new earth, which gives meaning to today's work, justice and culture."}},
views:{
"joseph-reveal":"Keller asked three questions of this climax in Genesis 50: why did Joseph weep, what did he say, and who is Joseph? Joseph could truly forgive because he said, \"Am I in the place of God?\": leaving judgment to God, he did not have to settle the score himself; and he saw God's purpose in his suffering: \"you meant evil against me, but God meant it for good, to bring it about that many people should be kept alive.\" Joseph, betrayed by his brothers and finally saving them, points to Jesus.",
"burning-bush":"Keller said Moses already believed in God, but only at the burning bush did he truly meet him. The passage gives three stages of finding God: an event that disrupts life and makes no sense; on coming closer, an unmanageable power; and in the midst of the fire, an angel. This God has a name: he is both consuming fire and personally near.",
"elijah-horeb":"Keller used this passage to show how God treats a servant sunk in depression, setting out three things God does for Elijah and three things Elijah learns. God is not in the wind, the earthquake or the fire, but speaks in a low whisper.",
esther:"Esther never mentions God once, yet Keller used it to preach \"the silent sovereignty of God\": God is always at work in spite of appearances; the world is obsessed with appearances, and God's work can free us from enslavement to the world's ways. The book answers a question: in morally, culturally and spiritually ambiguous situations, does God still work with us?",
furnace:"Keller split the image and the furnace into two problems: the golden image is the problem of \"tolerance,\" an empire demanding that everyone worship together; the furnace is the problem of unjust suffering. He placed the passage in a series on theophanies: the fourth man in the fire, \"like a son of the gods,\" is an anticipation of Jesus in the Old Testament. The friends' \"but if not\" faith prepares people to face any fiery trial.",
samson:"Keller said Samson reflected Israel's tendency to do what was right in their own eyes: he thought his strength was his own rather than from God. Samson is \"the broken victor\": he defeats the enemy in his death. Judges does not ask us to copy these heroes but shows our need for a better Judge.",
gideon:"Gideon was fearful and kept asking for signs, yet God still used him to save Israel, showing that God can work through imperfect vessels. But Keller also pointed out that late in life Gideon began \"ruling as a king\"; the ephod he made became a snare and set the stage for the tragedy of Abimelech in the next chapter.",
eden:"Keller pointed out that one of the main subjects of Genesis 1 and 2 is work: God forms the man with his own hands, plants the garden himself, and puts the man in it \"to work it and keep it.\" Work comes before the fall; it is part of what humans were made for, not a curse. After the fall work becomes toil, but it is not taken away. He also noted that the Bible begins with a wedding (Gen 2) and ends with the marriage supper of the Lamb (Rev 19).",
fall:"Keller saw four features of temptation in Genesis 3: the sneer (\"Did God really say?\"), the lie (God is not for you), the tree (wanting to decide good and evil yourself), and God's call (\"Where are you?\"). If human beings were basically good and the problem lay only in the environment, he said, there would be no cure; but the Bible says evil is not natural, it came in later, and that is why there is hope. Adam and Eve lost their oneness with God, with their true selves and with each other, and we lose it the same way today.",
cain:"Keller said Cain's murder of Abel is not just the first homicide but the first case study of life \"east of Eden\": in every part of life there is sin, there is grace, and there is the possibility of salvation. The second half of Genesis 4 is a tale of two cities: Cain builds a city to secure himself and make a name, producing a culture of death; yet cities are not evil in themselves but centers of creativity and culture, and God means to build a city founded on worship and grace.",
pentecost:"Keller read Pentecost against the background of Sinai: the first Pentecost had a mountain (Sinai), a ministry (the law of Moses) and a new people (Israel); Acts 2 has a new mountain, a new ministry and a whole new people. The message of that day is for every nation.",
"rev-white-throne":"Keller held that understanding what the Bible says about hell is key to understanding your own heart. In the parable of the rich man and Lazarus, the poor man has a name and the rich man does not: the rich man made \"his good things\" the source of his identity and worth, and in the end lost himself. Believing that God will finally judge justly, he said, frees people from taking revenge themselves so they can live at peace; and seeing the reality of hell shows what Jesus bore for us on the cross, and how deep God's love is.",
annunciation:"Keller presented Mary as a model of responsive faith: first she asks honestly, \"How will this be?\", not blindly; then she submits, \"I am the servant of the Lord; let it be to me according to your word\"; finally she praises (the Magnificat). Faith is not the absence of questions but entrusting yourself, questions and all, to God's word.",
magi:"\"Where is he who has been born king of the Jews?\" The question unsettles Herod, because this King's kingdom threatens everyone who wants to be in charge. Keller said each of us has a \"little Herod\" inside who will not give up the throne. Foreigners from far away come to worship, while those in Jerusalem who knew the Scriptures do not move.",
incarnation:"The doctrine of Christmas is the incarnation: the infinite God became a finite human, fully God and fully man. So God is not distant; he enters our situation himself. The light shines into the world from outside it; the world does not produce it.",
baptism:"At Jesus' baptism Father, Son and Spirit appear together: the Father says \"You are my beloved Son\" and the Spirit descends. Keller described the Trinity as a \"dance\": mutual, self-giving love and glorifying of one another. We were made to enter that dance, but sin makes us stand still and demand that everyone revolve around us.",
temptation:"The baptism and the temptation are often read apart; Keller said they belong together. Having just heard the Father call him beloved Son, Jesus faces Satan's challenge to that identity: \"If you are the Son of God...\" Where Adam failed in the garden and Israel in the wilderness, Jesus wins in the wilderness, and with the words of Deuteronomy.",
"first-disciples":"Jesus does not wait, like other rabbis, for students to come to him; he calls them: \"Follow me.\" The call does not ask people to improve themselves first, but to trust him and come. Keller borrowed the invisible thread from George MacDonald's The Princess and the Goblin as a picture of following Jesus, often into places we cannot yet see.",
cana:"The question of this chapter is how the world will be put right. Running out of wine at a wedding shamed the whole village, and Jesus turns water for ritual washing into the best wine. Wine is the joy of the kingdom's feast; \"my hour has not yet come\" points to the cross: he is the bridegroom who will pay with himself so that we can sit at the feast.",
transfiguration:"Before heading to the cross, the disciples see Jesus' true glory. Peter wants to build tents to hold on to the moment; the Father says only, \"Listen to him.\" Keller pointed out that people are changed not by effort but by beholding the glory of Christ.",
"temple-cleansing":"Jesus' cleansing of the temple is not just about trade: the Court of the Gentiles was full of market stalls, and \"a house of prayer for all nations\" had become a place that kept people out. Keller read the cursing of the fig tree together with the cleansing: the old temple system is ending, and Jesus himself is the new temple.",
"last-supper":"The Last Supper is a new Passover: once the lamb's blood made judgment pass over Israel; now Jesus says this bread is my body and this cup my blood. The Lord's Supper looks back to the cross and forward to the final feast, a foretaste of it.",
gethsemane:"In Gethsemane Jesus is overwhelmed not merely by fear of physical death but by the \"cup\" he must drink: in the Old Testament, the cup of God's wrath and judgment. He is about to be cut off from the Father. He drinks the cup of judgment so that we can lift the cup of salvation.",
crucifixion:"Darkness at noon and the cry \"My God, why have you forsaken me?\": in that moment he bears being forsaken by God. The temple curtain is torn from top to bottom, showing that the way into God's presence is open; and the first to recognize him as the Son of God is the Gentile centurion who carried out the execution.",
emmaus:"The risen Jesus begins with Moses and the Prophets and shows that all of Scripture is about him (Luke 24:27). Keller made this the core principle of preaching: every passage should be read through to Christ, not as Old Testament moral tales but as pointing to the true and better Savior.",
"early-church":"In the early church \"there was not a needy person among them\"; Keller saw this as the fulfillment of the ideal in Deuteronomy 15: people changed by grace become generous with money and care for the poor. Justice is not added on to the gospel; it grows out of grace.",
zacchaeus:"Zacchaeus treated money as the source of his security and identity, a \"counterfeit god.\" Keller pointed out that Jesus did not demand change first but came to his house as a guest; once accepted by grace, Zacchaeus himself offered to give half his goods to the poor. The idol of money is not broken by willpower but displaced by a greater love.",
"second-journey":"Paul's speech on the Areopagus in Athens is Keller's main example of \"contextualization\": he first understands his audience and quotes their poets, affirming what is right; then shows that their idols cannot fulfill their own hopes; finally he calls them to repent and points to the risen Christ. Enter the culture, challenge the culture, appeal to the culture."}
};

window.EN_ISSUES={
creation:{n:"Creation and evolution",q:"What question is Genesis 1 answering? Can human origins and evolutionary science coexist?",p:"Genesis 1 answers \"who\" and \"why\" (God created; humans bear his image), not modern science's \"how\" and \"when\"; first ask what the original readers needed to know.",k:"The genre of Genesis 1 is closer to poetic narrative and need not oppose every form of evolutionary biology; but he rejected evolution as a worldview that explains everything, and insisted that Adam and Eve were historical and the fall a historical event, or Paul's teaching on salvation cannot stand.",o:{lewis:"Could accept that the human body was prepared through a long process, but insisted on a real fall of humanity.",kidner:"Proposed a model: Adam can be understood as a historical person chosen by God as the covenant head of humanity."},agree:"Genesis 1 is about theology rather than a scientific timetable; the fall is real history.",differ:"Keller explicitly held that evolutionary science and faith can coexist; we have not yet found a citable statement of Pawson's position on this."},
baptism:{n:"Baptism",q:"Who should be baptized? Is baptism only a sign, or a real experience?",p:"Believers' baptism. Baptism is not merely an outward sign but a real experience of dying, being buried and rising with Christ, one of four steps of \"the normal Christian birth\": repent, believe, be baptized, receive the Spirit.",k:"The Presbyterian position: adults are baptized when they come to faith, and the children of believers are baptized too, because they belong to the covenant community; in the new covenant baptism takes over circumcision's role as covenant sign.",o:{packer:"Supported infant baptism on covenant grounds, seeing baptism as the sign and seal of God's promise."},agree:"Baptism is not an optional ritual; it is tied to union with Christ.",differ:"Who is baptized (believers only, or believers and their children), and how baptism relates to regeneration and receiving the Spirit."},
spirit:{n:"Baptism in the Spirit and spiritual gifts",q:"Is receiving the Spirit automatic at conversion, or a distinct experience? Do miraculous gifts continue today?",p:"Receiving the Spirit is a clearly experienced event and does not necessarily happen automatically at conversion (Acts 8, 19); the gifts of the Spirit still operate in the church today.",k:"Every Christian has a set of gifts from the Spirit for serving others; but gifts cannot replace fruit: someone can influence others through gifts while their spiritual life declines. In his sermon series Knowing the Holy Spirit he warned that some churches stress only the Spirit and others only doctrine, and both need balance; he named three marks of being filled with the Spirit: praise in the heart, contentment that gives thanks in everything, and mutual submission out of reverence for Christ (cf. Eph 5:18-21). We have not found his direct statement on whether \"baptism in the Spirit\" is a second experience.",o:{chan:"Argued that today's church has forgotten the Holy Spirit and faith runs on human effort.",packer:"Affirmed the vitality of the charismatic movement while offering careful criticism of its theology."},agree:"The Spirit's work is real, and gifts are for service.",differ:"Pawson stresses receiving the Spirit as an experienced, distinct event; Keller's focus is the balance of gifts and character."},
perseverance:{n:"Can salvation be lost?",q:"Can a true believer lose salvation? To whom are the New Testament warnings addressed?",p:"The New Testament warnings are addressed to believers and are real: a believer who persists in unbelief and abandons Christ can lose salvation, so we must persevere to the end. Revelation says the overcomer's name will not be blotted out of the book of life, which in reverse is a warning.",o:{packer:"Held the Reformed doctrine of the perseverance of the saints: God will complete the work he began, and true believers will persevere to the end."},agree:"Both sides say faith must be held fast, and \"being saved\" is no license to sin.",differ:"Pawson held believers can fall away; the Reformed view (Packer) holds true believers will be kept. We have not yet found a source for Keller's direct statement on this question."},
millennium:{n:"Millennium and rapture",q:"Is the thousand years of Revelation 20 literal? Will the church go through the great tribulation?",p:"Historic premillennialism with a post-tribulation rapture: the church goes through the great tribulation; when the Lord returns believers are caught up to meet him and immediately come down with him to reign on earth a thousand years. He saw the pre-tribulation rapture as a 19th-century innovation.",o:{lewis:"Stressed that the Second Coming is certain and sudden and rejected date-setting; the point is to be ready at all times."},agree:"Christ will certainly return in person; history has an end.",differ:"We have not yet found a source for Keller's direct statement on the millennium."},
israel:{n:"The future of Israel",q:"Does ethnic Israel still have a role in God's plan? Who is meant in Romans 11:26?",p:"Romans 9–11 is the climax of the letter: \"all Israel will be saved\" means ethnic Israel turning to the Messiah at the end. He saw the modern rebirth of Israel as the start of the \"nation first, then spiritual life\" sequence of Ezekiel 37.",o:{},agree:"",differ:"We have not yet found a source for Keller's direct statement on this question."},
law:{n:"Law, Sabbath and the new covenant",q:"Must Christians keep the law of Moses? How is the Sabbath kept today?",p:"Christians are not under the law of Moses (including its Sabbath rules) but under \"the law of Christ\"; the Mosaic covenant was given to Israel and has been replaced by the new covenant, though its moral principles are restated in the New Testament.",k:"The Sabbath principle still matters: weekly rest from work is God's gift; but the deeper rest is ceasing from the burden of justifying ourselves by our performance and entering Christ's finished work.",o:{packer:"Surveyed the Puritan view of the Lord's Day, affirming it as a day of worship and rest."},agree:"Salvation comes before the law (exodus before Sinai); obedience responds to grace.",differ:"Pawson does not treat the Sabbath as a rule for Christians; Keller keeps weekly rest as a principle of practice."},
hell:{n:"Hell and judgment",q:"Is hell eternal? How can a loving God judge people?",p:"Defended eternal punishment and rejected annihilationism, noting that most of Jesus' warnings about hell were spoken to disciples.",k:"Hell is the continuation and end of a person's ongoing choice to center life on themselves and away from God; in the end God respects that choice. He drew on Lewis to answer how a loving God can allow hell.",o:{lewis:"\"The doors of hell are locked on the inside\": hell is God respecting a person's final rejection of him.",chan:"After re-examining Scripture, concluded that hell cannot be removed from Christian faith."},agree:"Both insist on eternal judgment.",differ:"The emphasis differs: Pawson stresses it as a warning to believers; Keller stresses hell as the result of people's own choice."},
gender:{n:"Gender roles in the church",q:"Can women serve as elders or senior pastors?",p:"Leadership in church and family belongs to men; Deborah is an exceptional case.",k:"Complementarian: the Redeemer Presbyterian Church he led did not ordain women as elders, but women served widely, including on the diaconate by commissioning rather than ordination.",o:{packer:"Opposed ordaining women as elders and pastors."},agree:"Both are complementarian: men and women equal in dignity, with the office of elder held by men.",differ:"Breadth of practice: Keller's church gave women more room to serve in non-ordained roles."},
suffering:{n:"Suffering and God's sovereignty",q:"Why do good people suffer? Where is God in suffering?",p:"Job challenges the idea that good people prosper and suffering always means sin. The friends' theology sounds right but is rebuked by God; what Job receives is not an explanation but an encounter with God.",k:"Suffering is not meaningless; God does not stand outside it but in Christ enters suffering himself. He used Job to show that pouring out grief, even complaint, to God is closer to faith than pretending all is well.",o:{lewis:"First dealt with the problem of pain rationally, then, after his wife's death, wrote of his struggle far more nakedly.",knechtle:"In campus Q&A often answers \"why does God allow evil\" from human freedom and the consequences of sin."},agree:"Suffering cannot be reduced to \"you must have sinned\"; God's answer is himself.",differ:""}
};

window.EN_ALT={creation:"Young-earth view: c. 4000 BC; old-earth view: the universe is c. 13.8 billion years old",flood:"Global flood, or a large regional flood",["abram-call"]:"Some scholars date it c. 1900–1800 BC",job:"Proposed dates of writing range from the patriarchal era to after the exile",passover:"Late date: c. 1270–1260 BC (under Rameses II)",jericho:"The archaeological dating is disputed (Kenyon and Wood differ)",jonah:"Genre debated: historical narrative or teaching narrative",["suffering-servant"]:"Critical scholarship: Isaiah 40–55 was written near the end of the exile (c. 540 BC)",["daniel-exile"]:"Critical scholarship: Daniel was written c. 165 BC",nativity:"Herod died in 4 BC, so Jesus was born before that",["john-baptist"]:"Luke 3:1, \"the fifteenth year of Tiberius,\" is about AD 28/29",["triumphal-entry"]:"The other main view: AD 33",galatians:"North Galatian view: c. AD 53–55",revelation:"Early date: c. AD 68 (under Nero)"};
